        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Yes, this has been discussed repeatedly, and the conclusion is always that we should not port the runtime for the sake of it -- the cost of decoupling from Vim's runtime maintenance is too high, and the vast majority of files would not benefit from this at all. The examples @superatomic cites all apply to Vim as well and therefore -- according to long-standing project policy -- **must** be solved by them.\n\nUnless upstream completely drops the ball on this and makes decisions we fundamentally disagree with so we go full divorce, not touching upstream runtime files remains the default, with exceptions for very specific cases, e.g.,\n1. upstream has (fully) rewritten them in vim9script themselves, and backporting further vim9script updates to vimscript classic is as much effort as to Lua,\n2. the upstream version is incompatible with some Neovim API and needs significant changes,\n3. a rewrite allows _significant_ (triple-digit) observable real-world performance improvements (like the filetype detection, which Vim later reinvented in vim9script).\n\nSo as a general \"let's rewrite runtime in Lua!\" project, this is still a hard \"No\", I'm afraid.",
            "created_at": "2026-07-14T08:34:00Z",
